WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.720
Was there a scholarship here for you?

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:10.480
Yeah, they had offered me a scholarship before, and I told them I was going to Tulane.

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.240
I guess I got here . . . Jess Tinsley, a cousin of mine,

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.440
had played about four or five years before at LSU and had been a good lineman for them.

5
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.460
So they got to talking to me about how great he was and all that stuff,

6
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.940
how I should stay here, so I just decided to stay at LSU.

7
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.580
So when you came, your scholarship, did it pretty much pay all your bills or was there . . . were there extra expenses?

8
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.440
No, there was . . . it paid all . . . all the bills, I mean, room, board, and tuition, and all that stuff.

9
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.260
And we usually got jobs in the summertime to help buy clothes and things.

10
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.320
What kinds of jobs around here?

11
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.120
All the way from working on the highway to oil field jobs.

12
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.740
Anything that was open.

13
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.340
And they . . . Of course, they usually helped us to find them.

14
00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.720
Would you go back home during the summertime, or did you stay in Baton Rouge?

15
00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.580
Not necessarily.

16
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.860
Sometimes we’d stay in Baton Rouge, sometimes the job would be back at home.

17
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.760
Your coaches helped to get these jobs or were these things you had to go out and catch on your own?

18
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.640
No, I guess it came through the Athletic Department at Baton Rouge.

19
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.120
I’m sure it came through the, you know, the Highway Department here in Baton Rouge.

20
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.580
And they would tell the people in that particular parish if they had a job or if they’d like to,

21
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.440
for them to help out the football players.

22
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.180
And I don’t remember . . . I have no idea what the amount of money was.

23
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.280
It wasn’t a big amount of money.

24
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.560
It was just a normal salary that you would have gotten for that work if you hadn’t been a football player.

25
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.980
But I do not remember.

26
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.740
I have no idea what it was.

27
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:46.760
What I’m wondering about is how did the Depression affect . . . I’m wondering if that affected your ability to come to LSU and stay or if it made it more of a struggle?

28
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.260
No, I . . . like I say, I don’t guess that it really did.

29
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.980
I mean, I guess it affected everybody, you know, some.

30
00:01:53.980 --> 00:02:00.460
But so far as what we . . . The real necessities of life during the stay at LSU,

31
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.620
we . . . we got that all right because as I say, the scholarship covered almost all of it.

32
00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.520
See, our scholarship covered room, board, laundry, books, and all that kind of stuff.

33
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:18.300
So we didn’t just . . . Actually the only expenses that we had were mainly clothes and just personal expenses,

34
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.680
you know, that we had to pay for.
